1://! The opencode configuration the pipeline's agents are resolved out of.
2://!
3://! `rbops` launches the reviewer as `opencode --agent reviewer`, and the
4://! implementer as `--agent build`. opencode resolves agent definitions from the
5://! project config first — `./opencode.json` at the working directory, walked up
6://! to the worktree root — and only then from `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`,
7://! with the project deep-merged over the global. Before this file existed the
8://! checkout declared no `agent` key anywhere in that path, so neither name
9://! resolved and both agents silently ran as the global `default_agent: build`,
10://! whose `edit` and `bash` are `allow`. The reviewer is contractually
11://! read-only; a permissions gap that makes it writable is invisible to every
12://! gate, because nothing the gate runs asks the reviewer to write something.
13://!
14://! Two things bind `--agent reviewer` to this file's agent, and both are
15://! asserted here:
16://!
17://! - the **permission block**, which decides what the reviewer may do; and
18://! - the **mode**, which decides whether the name resolves to this agent at
19://!   all. opencode binds `--agent <name>` to a *primary* agent: a name that
20://!   resolves to a subagent makes opencode print `agent "reviewer" is a
21://!   subagent, not a primary agent. Falling back to default agent` and run the
22://!   default agent instead — here `build`, whose `edit` and `bash` are
23://!   `allow`. A permission block that is never bound is not a permission
24://!   block.
25://!
26://! `agent.verifier` is declared here, but no `--agent verifier` exists in
27://! `rbops` today — the pipeline runs its gates as a script, not as an agent. It
28://! is declared because a dispatcher that runs a phase's gates as an agent must
29://! resolve to *these* permissions rather than to the global build agent, and
30://! because the declaration is what makes that wiring a one-word change instead
31://! of a second permissions hole. The tests below pin the declaration; they do
32://! not claim the pipeline launches it.
33://!
34://! These tests read the shipped file and assert its contents. That is the whole
35://! claim they can make: the opencode config schema is documented at
36://! <https://opencode.ai/config.json>, and the resolution order above is
37://! documented in opencode's configuration reference
38://! (<https://opencode.ai/docs/config>, section "Locations") and in the
39://! `customize-opencode` skill opencode ships (section "Where files live").
40://! A test can prove the file says the right thing; it cannot prove which file
41://! opencode opened. What it *can* do is fail the moment a `deny` is dropped,
42://! a `deny` is narrowed to a pattern that leaves the rest of the paths to the
43://! rules above it, an agent is renamed, a mode is changed, or a
44://! `default_agent` is quietly reintroduced to repoint the implementer agent.
45://!
46://! Two further rules keep the file from reaching past the agent it is about.
47://! opencode deep-merges the project config over the global one, per key, so
48://! both of these would move a permission for an agent the file does not
49://! declare: a **top-level `permission` block**, which applies to every agent
50://! that declares no block of its own, and an **agent this file does not own**
51://! — `build` above all, since the pipeline runs every phase's implementer as
52://! `--agent build` and a block for that name here would replace the global
53://! one for it.
54://!
55://! The same reach is closed by two ALLOW-LISTS, one per level of the
56://! document: the top-level keys the file may declare (`$schema` and
57://! `agent`), and the keys an agent block may declare (`description`, `mode`
58://! and `permission`). `instructions` is the sharp one at the top level — it
59://! is prepended to the system prompt of every agent in the project — and
60://! `prompt` is the same reach one level down: it is the agent's own prompt,
61://! so a line there is a line the reviewer reads as an instruction from the
62://! pipeline rather than from its own contract.
63://!
64://! The reviewer's permission block is closed as well, in both directions: it
65://! declares **exactly** the eleven keys it must deny. `read` is not one of
66://! them, and declaring it would be a widening rather than a statement of
67://! intent — opencode's default `read` rules ask about `.env` files, and an
68://! agent-level `read: "allow"` is appended after them and wins the
69://! last-match.
