79:/// Every permission key the reviewer must deny, and why each one is named.
80:///
81:/// The list is opencode's own key set. The schema types `AgentConfig.permission`
82:/// with `read`, `edit`, `glob`, `grep`, `list`, `bash`, `task`,
83:/// `external_directory`, `todowrite`, `question`, `webfetch`, `websearch`,
84:/// `lsp`, `doom_loop` and `skill`; every one of them that is not a read-only
85:/// tool is here.
86:///
87:/// - `edit` covers `write`, `edit` and `apply_patch` — one key, not three.
88:/// - `bash` is the shell the reviewer has no business running.
89:/// - `task` is what stops the reviewer from delegating to a subagent, and the
90:///   built-in `general` subagent has full tool access.
91:/// - `webfetch` and `websearch` are the two ways out of the checkout: one is
92:///   granted by the global config, the other by opencode's permissive default.
93:/// - `skill` loads a skill file into the reviewer's context. A skill is
94:///   instructions the reviewer then follows, and it can be fetched from disk
95:///   or from a URL — a grant, not a read.
96:/// - `lsp` runs language-server queries. The reviewer has no use for it and no
97:///   reason to hold it.
98:/// - `question` asks a human. A pipeline review has no human to ask, and the
99:///   answer would be the only way to widen the reviewer.
100:/// - `todowrite` writes to the session, not to the tree — but it is still a
101:///   grant, and the reviewer is granted nothing it does not need.
102:/// - `doom_loop` is opencode's repeated-call guard. It defaults to `ask`, and
103:///   the pipeline runs `opencode run` with nobody to answer a prompt, so it is
104:///   denied outright: the guard blocks the repeated call instead of waiting
105:///   for an answer that will never come.
106:/// - `external_directory` is the one the global config grants as
107:///   `{"**": "allow"}`.
108:///
109:/// opencode's defaults are permissive ("Most permissions default to `allow`"),
110:/// so a key that is not declared is a key that is allowed.
