125:/// The permission keys the reviewer must not declare AT ALL.
126:///
127:/// These are the read-only tools its job needs, and opencode already allows
128:/// them by default. The default is the safer of the two answers available
129:/// here, so the shipped file declares none of them — and the checker refuses
130:/// any of them, whatever value they carry.
131:///
132:/// Declaring `read: "allow"` would be an *widening* rather than a statement
133:/// of intent: opencode's default `read` rules ASK about `.env` files
134:/// (`read *.env ask`), and an agent-level `read: "allow"` is appended after
135:/// them, so it would win the last-match and re-allow them. Declaring
136:/// `read: "deny"` would take the reviewer's own job away — it is there to
137:/// read the diff. The default is the safer of the two here: the pipeline
138:/// runs `opencode run` with nobody to answer a prompt, so an `ask` is a
139:/// request that cannot be granted — the same reason `doom_loop` is denied
140:/// outright rather than left to ask.
